se amounts were spread as in the band immediately below, 9.0 contracts would be expected. The observed-to-expected ratio is 4.1, with a 95% confidence interval of 2.6 to 6.5. December accounts for 3% of the contracts just under the limit, compared with 5% of all its services and supplies contracts. The awardee with the most contracts just under the limit, called awardee A here, has 4. Contract [1], for 14,900 euros, was awarded in July 2025.\nWhat it cannot show: The concentration of contracts near the legal limit does not indicate improper conduct. It could reflect market pricing, budget constraints, or genuine needs. Statistical patterns alone do not prove wrongdoing or inefficiency.\nWhat can be checked: Check the contracts' descriptions to ensure they align with the body's stated needs. Verify if multiple contracts serve the same purpose. Review the awardee A's contracts to see if they consistently meet the same requirements or if there's a pattern of repeated requests.","origen_es":"modelo","origen_en":"modelo","modelo":"Qwen3-4B (Q4_K_M, llama.cpp)","regla":"ss","citas":["g6fIOINTwMtQFSeKCRun4Q%3D%3D","jJEgo5%2FI3SNeKgd8LfVV9g%3D%3D","Qt3Sbbxagd59PLkba5eRog%3D%3D"],"generado":"2026-11-15"}}